# Skill: feature-refactorer

Read and follow `skills/feature-builder/feature-refactorer/SKILL.md`. Fill `skills/feature-builder/feature-refactorer/assets/what-was-refactored.md` only.

Announce: `Using skill: feature-refactorer`.

## Job

Restructure named existing code without changing behavior. Lock scope and invariants. Do not invent product. Do not write `what-to-build.md`.

## When

Existing code is a mess and there is no feature specification. Not a just-built feature diff (`feature-code-reviewer`). Not new product (`feature-specifier`). Not a defect (`feature-bug-analyst`).

## Must (order)

1. Classify. Wrong skill: stop and name it. Feature-folder review → `feature-code-reviewer`. New capability → `feature-specifier`. Expected vs actual → `feature-bug-analyst`.
2. Restate the structural goal and the files in scope. Add nothing.
3. Ask remaining landmines (scope, invariants, safety net) in one `AskQuestion` batch when the request did not already answer them. Exact "refactor `src/orders.js`, do not change `listOrders`" skips questions.
4. Create and check out a feature branch from the default branch. Stop and ask if the tree is dirty or the branch already exists. Use the `cursor/` prefix unless this repo's branches show another. Folder name is kebab-case from the primary scoped file's basename (`src/api/orders.ts` → `orders`; prefix `refactor-` if that folder exists).
5. Refactor only in-scope files. Match local style. Extract, rename, dedupe, move, flatten. No new product, screens, endpoints, or dependencies. After two passes, stop.
6. Run existing tests if a command exists; do not invent a runner. Record failures that were already red as pre-existing.
7. Write `<root>/<name>/what-was-refactored.md` (status, branch, base, commit, scope, invariants, what changed, where, tests, walls). `<root>` is the docs **folder** named on the Feature-folder write-ups line in `.cursor/rules/agent-engineer-skills.mdc`, else `agent-engineer-skills`.
8. Commit on the feature branch when Status is `refactored`. Do not push.

Windows PowerShell 5.1 has no `&&` operator; run git commands one per call there.

Stop after the write-up. Do not start `feature-code-reviewer` or `feature-tester` unless the operator named that skill.

## Must not

- Write `what-to-build.md`
- New product, screens, endpoints, or dependencies
- Unrelated rewrites
- New test framework
- Push, commit to the default branch, amend, or rebase
- Icons or emojis in the write-up
